INT. COURTHOUSE ELEVATOR - DAY

Stainless steel. Mirrored door. Four floors of descent.

NKIRU EZEIGBO, thirties, defence attorney, bangs her briefcase against her knee. WYATT MERRIWEATHER, forties, prosecutor, studies the floor numbers. TOMASZ KOWAL, fifties, bailiff, keeps both hands on his belt.

The car shudders. Lights flicker. The elevator stops between floors.

NKIRU
Of course.

WYATT
It does this.

TOMASZ
Fourth time this month.

Nkiru's cell phone buzzes. She answers on speaker.

JUDGE REYNOSO (V.O.)
(over phone)
Counsel. Where are you?

NKIRU
Stuck, Your Honour.

JUDGE REYNOSO (V.O.)
Both of you?

WYATT
Mr. Kowal is with us.

JUDGE REYNOSO (V.O.)
I'm calling the jury. You have ten minutes.

NKIRU
Wyatt, we need to settle.

WYATT
I know.

NKIRU
Then why did you reject my offer?

WYATT
Because you didn't make one.

NKIRU
I'm making one now.

WYATT ^
I'm making one now.

Silence. They glare at each other in the mirror.

TOMASZ
For what it's worth, I'd take it.

Lights return. The elevator drops with a lurch. All three grab the rail.
